course: everyday
lesson: 50
title: Thinking like a lifeguard
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Waves push water up onto the beach all the time, and that water has to flow back out to sea somewhere; it gathers and rushes back through a narrow gap, often a dip between sandbanks, and that fast outward stream is a rip, so the waves come in while the rip goes out.
explain: A rip is usually narrow, often only a few house-widths wide, so swimming sideways for a short way gets you out of it, while swimming straight against it can be like running the wrong way on a moving walkway.
explain: Lightning usually strikes the tallest thing around, so a lone tree in a field is the worst shelter: the strike can jump from the trunk to a person or spread through the ground; a building or a hard-topped car is safe, because the car's metal body carries the current around the outside, not because of the rubber tyres.
explain: Sound travels about one kilometre in three seconds, while the light of a flash arrives almost at once, so counting the seconds from flash to thunder and dividing by three tells you about how many kilometres away the storm is.
explain: In London the nearest cars come from the right, so a boy from New York who looks left first, out of habit, sees an empty lane and steps into the one where the danger really is.
explain: A lifeguard at a pool in Florida saw a child go still under the water while others were playing; she pulled him out within seconds, and he was fine because nobody waited.
explain: A father in Ireland jumped into a river to save his son's dog; the cold water and current were too strong for him, and both had to be rescued by firefighters.
fact: Water pushed onto the beach by waves must flow back out to sea.
fact: A rip forms where that water rushes back out through a narrow gap, such as a deeper dip between sandbanks, or beside rocks or a pier.
fact: Fewer waves break over a rip, because the water there is deeper and flowing out, so a rip looks calmer.
fact: Most rips are narrow, so a short swim parallel to the shore takes you out of them.
fact: Lightning usually strikes the tallest object in the area.
fact: Standing under a lone tree in a thunderstorm is dangerous.
fact: A car with a metal roof protects you from lightning because the metal body carries the current around you.
fact: Rubber tyres are not what makes a car safe in lightning.
fact: Light arrives almost at once, but sound takes about three seconds to travel one kilometre.
fact: If you hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning, so you go indoors.
fact: In left-driving countries the nearest lane of traffic comes from your right.
fact: Habits from home can be dangerous in a country where traffic drives on the other side.
fact: Fluorescent colours glow brightly in daylight, but reflective material is what shines back in headlights at night.
fact: Many people who drown are trying to save someone else; a panicking person in the water can grab a rescuer and pull them under.
fact: Seconds matter in a water emergency, so watching closely and acting fast saves lives.
exercise: Waves keep rolling in to the beach. Why is there still a current going out? | The water the waves bring in has to flow back out somewhere | The Moon pulls the water back out to sea every few minutes, like a tap | Wind under the water blows the other way | Fish swimming out to sea drag the water
exercise: Why do rips often form in a dip between sandbanks? | The returning water flows out through the deepest, easiest gap | Sandbanks push water towards the beach only | Dips in the sand are where the water is always warmest, so it rises up and out | Sand makes the water heavier there
exercise: Why does swimming parallel to the shore work against a rip? | Rips are narrow, so a short swim sideways takes you out | Rips flow sideways along the beach, so you swim along with them | Sideways swimming makes you lighter | The current stops when you turn
exercise: Swimming against a rip is like what? | Running the wrong way on a moving walkway | Swimming in a still pool | Riding a bike downhill | Floating on your back in a warm bath at home
exercise: Why is a lone tree in a field a bad shelter in a thunderstorm? | Lightning tends to strike the tallest thing and can jump to you | Trees attract rain, so you get wetter | The leaves block out the sound of thunder | Lightning never strikes the same tree twice, so a lone tree has already been hit
exercise: Why are you safe from lightning inside a hard-topped car? | The metal body carries the current around you | The rubber tyres stop the electricity reaching you | Lightning cannot see inside cars | Glass windows bounce the lightning away
exercise: You see a flash, then hear thunder 9 seconds later. About how far away is the storm? | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 27 kilometres | Right above you
exercise: Why do you see lightning before you hear thunder? | Light travels far faster than sound | Thunder is made after the lightning has finished | Your eyes work faster than your ears | Sound has to go round the clouds
exercise: A boy hears thunder but sees no rain yet. Should he keep playing football outside? | No, if he can hear thunder, lightning can reach him | Yes, lightning only ever strikes where it is already raining | Yes, as long as he stays away from the goal | No, but only if the thunder is very loud
exercise: Why should a girl from Boston look right first when crossing in Dublin? | Traffic drives on the left, so the nearest cars come from the right | Traffic in Dublin always comes from the right only, on every street in the city | Irish cars drive faster on the right | Looking right first is polite in Ireland
exercise: Why is a habit from home dangerous to a visitor crossing roads abroad? | He looks first at the far lane and misses the near one | Habits make him walk too slowly | Visitors are not allowed to use the crossings in a country that is not their own | Drivers abroad do not stop for visitors
exercise: A child wears a fluorescent orange jacket with no reflective strips on a dark night. Why is she still hard to see? | Fluorescent colours need daylight; at night only reflectors shine back | Orange cannot be seen by drivers at all | The jacket glows only when it is raining | Headlights make fluorescent colours look black, so the child disappears in the dark
exercise: Why does a rip often look calmer than the water next to it? | Fewer waves break where the water is flowing out deep and fast | The rip is shallower than the water around it, so the waves pass over it | Rips happen only on windless days | Seaweed in the rip flattens the waves
exercise: Why is it smarter to float when you are tired in a rip than to fight it? | Floating saves strength until the rip weakens or help comes | Floating makes the rip turn round and carry you gently back towards land | Fighting it makes the rip pull you under | Floating makes you sink below the current
test: A girl in a thunderstorm on a golf course in Florida can run to the clubhouse or to a big lone oak. Which is safer and why? | The clubhouse, because a lone tall tree draws lightning | The oak, because its leaves keep her dry | The oak, because lightning avoids living things | Either, because lightning never strikes the same place
test: A family on a camping trip counts 15 seconds between a flash and its thunder. About how far away is the lightning? | About 5 kilometres | About 15 kilometres | About 45 kilometres | About half a kilometre
test: Which is true about a car in a lightning storm? | Its metal body carries the current around the people inside | Its rubber tyres stop the lightning from reaching the ground at all | An open-topped car is just as safe | Cars attract lightning and should be left at once
test: On a beach with big waves, where would you expect the strongest flow out to sea? | In a narrow gap where fewer waves are breaking | Where the biggest, loudest waves are crashing onto the sand | Right at the edge of the dry sand | Only far out beyond the horizon
test: A boy from Toronto steps off the kerb in Auckland after looking left and seeing nothing. Which lane might have a car he missed? | The nearest lane, with cars coming from his right | The far lane, with cars coming from his left | No lane, because Auckland roads are one way | The cycle lane, as cars do not use the near lane
test: A swimmer is pulled out by a rip but stays calm, floats and then swims across the beach. Why did this work? | The rip was narrow, so the sideways swim got her out | The rip turned around to bring her home | Calm swimmers are not felt by the current, so it lets them go | Floating made the water stop moving
test: If there were no gaps between sandbanks along a beach, where could a rip still form? | Wherever the returning water finds an easier way out, such as beside rocks or a pier | Nowhere, because rips need sandbanks to exist and cannot form without them on any beach | Only in the middle of the open ocean | Only on beaches with no waves at all
test: Why is a fluorescent vest useful for a road worker in the day but not enough at night? | Fluorescent colours glow in daylight; at night reflectors return the headlights | Fluorescent colours glow brighter at night, when headlights shine on them from far away | Road workers only work in the daytime | Reflectors work only in sunshine
test: Thunder and lightning come at the same moment. What does this tell you? | The storm is right overhead and very dangerous | The storm is far away and harmless | The storm is moving away fast | Lightning and thunder have mixed up their order today
test: why is swimming sideways out of a rip enough | Most rips are narrow | Rips pull you under | Rips stop sideways | The sea is flatter sideways
test: storm on the golf course, under the big tree? | No, lightning hits tall things | Yes, trees are safe | Yes, the leaves keep you dry | Yes, lie under it
test: in the car in the storm, are we safe | Yes, the metal body carries the current around us | Yes, because of the rubber tyres, they stop it | No, cars attract lightning | No, open the windows
test: visiting london from texas, cross like at home? | No, look right first there | Yes, left first everywhere | Yes, roads are the same | Only look ahead
test: our dog's in the river and can't get out!! | Do not go in, call an adult or emergency services | Jump in and grab him | Swim out with a rope tied round your waist, it's fine | Wade in up to your chest
test: kid at the pool has gone quiet with his head low in the water | Shout for the lifeguard straight away | Wait and see if he is just playing a game | Splash him to wake him | Swim over and laugh
test: Max says good swimmers can always save someone who's drowning. true? | No, a panicking person can pull a rescuer under | Yes, good swimmers are always safe | Yes, if they jump in fast | Yes, unless the water is cold
test: heard a guy got struck by lightning cos he stood under the only tree in the field to stay dry | Real lesson: lightning often strikes the tallest thing, so never shelter under a lone tree | Real lesson: trees only get struck when it rains, so stand under them before the rain starts | It proves fields are always safe without trees | It is gossip, so lone trees are good shelter
test: my cousin reckons ur safe in a car in a storm cos the rubber tyres stop the lightning | Wrong reason; the metal body carries the current around you, not the tyres | He is right, rubber tyres are the reason | He is right, so a soft-top car with rubber tyres is just as safe | He is right, bikes have rubber tyres too
test: Jayden got pulled out by a lifeguard at the beach and people say he can't swim, pass it on | Do not pass it on; strong swimmers get caught in rips too, and it would embarrass him | Pass it on, so he takes lessons | Pass it on, because it is true | Pass it on, and add that he panicked and screamed so loud the whole beach heard him
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of a rip current? | Water pushed ashore by waves rushes back out to sea through a narrow gap | Wind blowing from the land pulls water out evenly along the whole length of the shore | Fish swimming together create a strong current | The tide draws water down towards the sea bed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a short swim parallel to the shore is effective in escaping a rip. | Most rips are narrow | Rips weaken when swimmers turn sideways | The sea is shallower along the shore | Lifeguards pull swimmers who swim sideways
exam: It being given that thunder is heard nine seconds after the flash, determine the approximate distance of the lightning. | About three kilometres | About nine kilometres | About thirty kilometres | Less than one hundred metres
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance where thunder is audible. | The person is close enough to be struck and should go indoors | The person is safe provided it is not raining | The person should shelter beneath the tallest tree that is near at hand | The person should lie down in an open field
